# Gaudium @VERSION@: install notes

This archive is Gaudium @VERSION@ for the Intel Gaudi 2 (HL-225), built for Linux x86_64
against release @SDK_RELEASE@ of the Intel Gaudi SDK.

Gaudium is in the middle of a port to Rust (see `README.md`): interfaces and flags still
change, and this is not a stable release.

## What is in the archive

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `bin/gaudium` | The engine and its HTTP server (one binary). |
| `kernels/` | Gaudium's TPC kernels (`*.o`) and its graph-compiler glue libraries (`libgaudium_*.so`), compiled with the `tpc-clang` of SDK @SDK_VERSION@. |
| `ui/` | The Web UI the server serves. |
| `docs/` | The command-line and environment reference, and the notes on the MCP proxy and HTTPS. |
| `BUILD-INFO.txt` | The commit and the tool versions of this build. |
| `SHA256SUMS` | The SHA-256 of every file above (`sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS`). |
| `LICENSE`, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` | Gaudium's licence (MIT) and the notices of the third-party code that is included. |

## What is not in it: the Intel Gaudi software

Gaudium runs on top of Intel's driver, firmware and SDK (SynapseAI). Their licence does not
allow redistribution, so **no file of them is in this archive: you install them yourself,
from Intel.**

- Install release **@SDK_RELEASE@** (package version `@SDK_VERSION@`): the kernels here were
  compiled for it. The packages are `habanalabs-dkms` (driver), `habanalabs-firmware` and
  `habanalabs-graph` (SynapseAI and the graph compiler); `habanalabs-tools` (`tpc-clang`) is
  only needed to compile the kernels yourself.
- Follow the [Intel Gaudi installation guide](https://docs.habana.ai/en/latest/Installation_Guide/index.html);
  the packages come from [Intel's package repository](https://vault.habana.ai/).
- With another SDK release, build Gaudium from source against that release (the "Building"
  section of `README.md`).

## Requirements

- A host with an Intel Gaudi 2 card and the Intel Gaudi software above.
- Linux x86_64 with glibc @GLIBC_MIN@ or later (this build was made on @OS_NAME@).
- A model checkpoint directory (see "Status" in `README.md` for the supported models).

## Install and run

```bash
sha256sum -c @NAME@.tar.gz.sha256        # next to the downloaded archive
tar -xzf @NAME@.tar.gz
cd @NAME@

bin/gaudium --version
bin/gaudium --help                       # every flag and environment variable
bin/gaudium                              # verified-core self-check (no card needed)
bin/gaudium --hpu-smoke                  # small live test on the card

bin/gaudium --serve --model <model_dir> --kernels "$PWD/kernels" --ui-dir "$PWD/ui" \
            --cache-dir <cache_dir> --max-ctx 32768    # API + Web UI on port 8090
```

`--cache-dir` keeps the compiled recipes between starts; without it every start compiles them
again. Use a new cache directory after installing another SDK release.

The engine loads `libSynapse.so` from `/usr/lib/habanalabs/` or the loader's search path
(set `GAUDIUM_SYNAPSE_LIB` to use another file). Only one process may open the card at a time.

The archive can be unpacked anywhere. `docs/reference.md` is the full `--help` text.
